        "score": 0.07763,
        "excerpt": "Department of Petroleum Engineering,  Heriot-Watt University  \n19\n44\nSemi-Infinite System Late Transient Analysis\n                             \no\nLnt\nLnt\nInp'\nDerivative Diagnostics\nCRD\nIntercept\nIdeal\nSemilog\nStraight Line\nLate time\nboundary\neffects\nWellbore\nstorage\nDerivative\nplateau\nETR\nMTR\nLTR\nETR\nMTR\nLTR\npwf\npt=1\nSlope, m\n-µq/(4π kh)\n                  \nThe late time region occurs when the bottom-hole pressure response of the well \nis influenced by the boundaries of the system.  These may be no-flow boundaries \narising from sealing faults or constant pressure upper or lower boundaries due to \na gas cap or aquifer.  The middle time region -  when it exists  - is associated with \ninfinite acting, radial flow behaviour and has the characteristic semilog slope of - \nqμ/4πkh.  The estimation of permeability from well testing depends crucially on the \ncorrect identification of the MTR.  The early time region is related to near well-bore \neffects such as limited entry or hydraulic fractures which cause deviation from \npure radial flow.  The definition of the ETR also includes the period during \nwhich wellbore storage affects the pressure response.  Analysis of the early time \nregion,"
