        "excerpt": "28\ntoo high.  Although this is corrected for in the MBH analysis it is still much preferable \nto employ the synthetic flowing time in the Horner plot.\nThe reservoir pressure at the external boundary, pe(tp) , is the forcing function which \n“drives” the buildup process. Even although the buildup will eventually level \noff at the average pressure, (tp) , during the early part the external pressure is \npe(tp) and this controls the initial buildup process.  Hence the extrapolation of \nthe early time behaviour, when the semilog straight line is manifested, yields this \ncontrolling boundary pressure pe(tp).\nThe preceding treatment was based on the case of a well in the centre of a circular \nreservoir which has the special feature that the infinite-acting time and the time \nto reach semi-steady-state are practically indistinguishable, ie. there is no late \ntransient. Consider now the case where the well is asymmetrically located in the \nbounded reservoir as shown in Figure 19.  During a period of constant rate drawdown \nfour periods can be identified:\n               \nFar Boundary\nNear Boundary\nWell\ntia\ntsia\npwf\nI.A.\nS.I.A\nIn t\nLate\nTransient\nSSS\n(1)   full infinite acting behaviour in whi"
